    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d90">

                <pb facs="0203" n="175" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v01/pg175"/>

                <head>90. Memorandum From the President’s Special Assistant for National Security
                    Affairs (Bundy) to the President<anchor n="1" xml:id="d90-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1964-03-18"><hi rendition="#italic">March 18,
                        1964</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Attached are two parting memoranda from Roger Hilsman to Dean Rusk which are
                    worth your attention when you have a chance to read them. With exceptions, I
                    think they are a good and clear assessment of the basic view of the matter which
                    this Government has had right along. Roger is a better analyst than
                    administrator, and this is the sort of thing he has done best. His specific
                    proposal that we put some <pb facs="0204" n="176" xml:id="pg176"/> troops in
                    Thailand is more attractive to State Department and White House staff than to
                    the Pentagon, because its object is political and not military. I think you may
                    hear more of this proposal in coming weeks.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">McG. B.</hi></signed></closer>



                <xi:include href="d90-a1.xml" xpointer="d90-a1"/>
                <xi:include href="d90-a2.xml" xpointer="d90-a2"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
